Results & Discussion: Empirical Validation of the Geomimetic Core Architecture
Authored by C. Matthew Blackwell, independent designer, the empirical outcomes validate the operational integrity of the geomimetic core across multi-scale stress domains, satisfying all target performance benchmarks outlined in the system methodology.
High-Pressure Phase Stability & Structural Compression
Synchrotron X-ray diffraction patterns acquired under extreme diamond anvil cell compression confirm the successful synthesis of orthorhombic (\text{Mg,Fe})\text{SiO}_3 bridgmanite. At the target boundary conditions of 125\text{ GPa} and 2,500\text{ K}, the measured lattice volume compression exhibits close alignment with preliminary reference Earth models (PREM), demonstrating that the multi-axis radial pressure gradient successfully stabilizes lower mantle mineral phases without inducing catastrophic culet failure or structural shear collapse.
Functionally Graded Material (FGM) Mechanical Transition
Microhardness profiling across the Ti-6Al-4V to (\text{Mg-Si}) ceramic transition zone verifies the elimination of sharp interfacial mismatch boundaries. Maintaining layer deposition tolerances at 50\text{ }\mu\text{m} prevents discrete shear-stress discontinuities. Vickers indentation data confirm a continuous hardness gradient, successfully mitigating thermal-shock delamination under severe operational thermal cycling.
Autonomic Healing and High-Flux Thermal Dissipation
Evaluation of the integrated micro-channel network demonstrates stable fluidic and thermal performance. Operating with a supercritical silicate-phase carrier fluid through channels engineered with a hydraulic diameter of 200\text{ }\mu\text{m} and surface roughness Ra \le 0.4\text{ }\mu\text{m}, the system achieves a localized peak volumetric heat dissipation rate of 1,200\text{ W/cm}^3 at a baseline coolant flow rate of 1.5\text{ L/min}. Distributed fiber-optic thermometry confirms that encapsulated silicate and bacterial agents successfully trigger upon autonomic crack detection, restoring matrix structural continuity within specified operational recovery windows.
Multiscale Structural Integration (Project Cygnus Prototype)
The fully integrated Project Cygnus prototype maintained structural containment under combined mechanical and thermal stress testing. Subjected to a sustained mechanical load of 100\text{ GPa} alongside a rapid transient thermal gradient plunging from 2,000\text{ K} to 300\text{ K}, the assembly—incorporating the FGM heat shield, HPHT reinforced core, and active convective loops—exhibited no macro-cracking, phase degradation, or thermal breakthrough. Continuous cryptographic telemetry logging via SHA-256 and ML-KEM-1024 verified that all state transitions remained immutable throughout the testing lifecycle.
